165	\begin{remark}[The commutation phase across basis indices]\label{rem:qld-cross-phase}
166		For $j \neq j'$ the source asserts that $\tilde{X}^j(\tilde{u})$ and $\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\tilde{v})$ commute for all $\tilde{u}, \tilde{v} \in \F_q^M$. Since $e_j e_{j'} \neq 0$ and the bilinear form $(a,b) \mapsto \tr(ab)$ on $\F_q$ is nondegenerate, there exist $\tilde{u}, \tilde{v}$ with $\tr(e_j e_{j'} (\tilde{u} \cdot \tilde{v})) = 1$, and for these \eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} yields anticommutation instead. By self-duality of the basis $\{e_j\}$, the dichotomy asserted by the source --- commutation for $j \neq j'$, and the phase $(-1)^{\tr((e_j\tilde{u}) \cdot (e_j\tilde{v}))}$ for $j = j'$ --- agrees with \eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} whenever $\tilde{u} \cdot \tilde{v} \in \{0,1\}$, in particular for all $\tilde{u}, \tilde{v} \in \{0,1\}^M$. The relations \eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} are not used in the remainder of the chapter.
167	\end{remark}
